======================================================================================================================
L19 -- does the gravitational-Cherenkov bound apply to IC10's shift-symmetric k-essence clock?
======================================================================================================================

======================================================================================================================
A -- the process, from first principles: kinematics and the emission rate
======================================================================================================================
    A primary of mass M and energy E, momentum p = sqrt(E^2 - M^2), emits one quantum of a mode
    with dispersion omega = v |k|, v < 1, at angle theta to its momentum.  Energy-momentum
    conservation with the final primary on shell gives
          cos(theta) = (E*v - k*(v**2 - 1)/2)/p   [= (E v + k(1-v^2)/2)/p]
          k_max      = (-2*E*v + 2*p)/(1 - v**2)         (the value at cos(theta) = 1)
          threshold  : k_max > 0  <=>  p > E v  <=>  E > M/sqrt(1-v^2)   [sympy: M*sqrt(-1/(v - 1))/sqrt(v + 1)]
          on shell   : 2 p.k = k**2*(v**2 - 1) = k^2_inv = -(1-v^2)|k|^2   (the emitted quantum is SPACELIKE, |q^2| = (1-v^2)|k|^2)

    The 1 -> 2 rate, with relativistic state normalisation and the azimuthal integral done:
          Gamma      = (1/(16 pi E p v)) Integral_0^k_max dk  |M|^2
          dE/dx      = (1/(16 pi E p))   Integral_0^k_max dk  k |M|^2      (each quantum carries v k)
          D_loss^-1  = (1/E) dE/dx                                          (the survival criterion)
  [PASS] C1 [CONTROL] the Cherenkov kinematics derive symbolically: cos(theta), k_max = 2(p - Ev)/(1-v^2), the threshold E > M/sqrt(1-v^2), and the on-shell identity 2 p.k = k^2 = -(1-v^2)|k|^2   (cos(theta) matches; k_max matches; threshold matches; 2p.k - k^2 = 0)

    Matrix-element controls, with explicit Dirac matrices and an explicit random F_mu_nu:
      Tr[(p'+M)(p+M)] = 10.6854380242  vs  4(p.p' + M^2) = 10.6854380242
      Maxwell T^mu_mu = 8.882e-16  (identically zero in 4D: a photon cannot emit a conformally
                        coupled scalar at tree level, whatever that scalar's speed)
  [PASS] C2 [CONTROL] Tr[(p'+M)(p+M)] = 4(p.p' + M^2) with explicit gamma matrices, and Maxwell's stress tensor is traceless identically   (Dirac trace rel. err 0.00e+00; Maxwell trace 8.88e-16)

======================================================================================================================
B -- CONTROL: the rate for a mode with GRAVITATIONAL-strength (tensor) coupling
======================================================================================================================
    Interaction L = -(kappa/2) h_mu_nu T^mu_nu with kappa = sqrt(32 pi G).  For a primary at E >> M
    the TT polarisation sum gives  Sum_pol |M|^2 = 2 p^4 sin^4(theta) / M_Pl^2  (M_Pl reduced), with
    sin^2(theta) from section A.  This is the standard structure Moore & Nelson and Elliott, Moore &
    Stoica use; the Lorentz-violating polarisation basis differs in O(1) factors, which is exactly
    what this control is checking.

    B.1  the analytic small-delta limit of the derived integral.  With M << E and delta = 1 - v << 1,
         sin^2(theta) = 2 delta (1 - k/E) - M^2/E^2 and k_max -> E, so
             D_loss^-1 = (1/E)(1/(16 pi E^2)) Int_0^E dk k 2E^4 [2 delta (1-k/E)]^2 / M_Pl^2
                       = delta^2 E^3/(24 pi M_Pl^2)  =  (1/3) G delta^2 E^3.
         Milgrom 2011 (arXiv:1102.1818) eq. (2) transcribes Moore & Nelson as D_loss^-1 = G p^3 Q,
         with Q ~ 1e-3 (n-1)^2 where n = 1/v; the 1e-3 is their PARTONIC factor (each parton carries
         ~0.1 p).  Stripping it, Q_coherent = delta^2, i.e. D_loss^-1 = G delta^2 E^3.
         numerically, from the full integral at delta = 1e-15, E = 3.0e+11 GeV:
             D_loss^-1 / (G delta^2 E^3) = 0.333333    (analytic 1/3 = 0.333333;  Moore & Nelson coherent: 1)

    B.2  the bound this rate imposes, with Moore & Nelson's own partonic factor 1e-03 and a 10 kpc path:
           E = 3.0e+11 GeV (3e20 eV, the energy MN quote):  1 - v <= 3.254e-15
           E = 1.0e+11 GeV (1e20 eV, this lane's brief):   1 - v <= 1.691e-14
         published (Moore & Nelson 2001, Galactic; the number L10 used):  1 - v <= 2.0e-15
         ratio derived/published at MN's energy: 1.63x
  [PASS] C3 [CONTROL] the tensor-coupled energy-loss rate derived here reproduces BOTH Moore & Nelson's coefficient (as transcribed by Milgrom 2011 eq. 2) and the published Galactic bound 1 - c <= 2e-15   (coefficient D_loss^-1/(G delta^2 E^3) = 0.3333 vs analytic 1/3 and MN-coherent 1 (agreement to a factor 1.0 / 3.0); derived Galactic bound 3.25e-15 vs published 2.0e-15, a factor 1.63. The machinery is calibrated against the literature it is about to be used to overturn)

======================================================================================================================
C -- what IC10's clock actually couples to, read off the action as published
======================================================================================================================
    IC10_LOCAL_CLOCK.md, the eta = 1 plateau (hand-transcribed):
        S10|eta=1 = Int sqrt(-gtilde) [ m* Rtilde/2 + P(Xtilde, w) ] + S_m[e^(2w) gtilde, psi],
        m* = m e^(-1/6),  Xtilde = -gtilde^{mu nu} T_mu T_nu / 2,  P_w = 0 the ALGEBRAIC w equation,
    and the frozen ingredient I2 (CRISPY_FRIED_CHICKEN_RECIPE.md): S_m = S_m[g, psi], ONE physical
    metric.  IC10 says the same: 'all ordinary matter still uses the single original physical metric g'
    with g = e^(2w) gtilde.

    C.1  THE STRUCTURAL FACT.  The clock field T does not appear in S_m AT ALL.  Matter sees only
         g = e^(2w) gtilde.  The clock reaches matter ONLY because w is slaved to Xtilde by P_w = 0.
         So the entire clock-matter vertex is carried by delta w, and delta w enters S_m as a
         CONFORMAL rescaling -- which couples to the TRACE of the matter stress tensor and to
         nothing else.  Derived symbolically, with a scalar matter field:
           d(S_m density)/dw = 2*(-2*V*exp(2*w) + Xtilde_psi)*exp(2*w)
           sqrt(-g)/sqrt(-gt) * T^mu_mu = 2*(2*V*exp(2*w) - Xtilde_psi)*exp(2*w)
           => dS_m/dw = -sqrt(-g) T^mu_mu   [exact]  -- the whole
           coupling, with no free structure left over.  The w field equation with matter present is
           therefore  P_w + e^(4w) T^mu_mu = 0, and matter's TRACE is the only thing that sources it.

    C.2  POSITIVE CONTROL: the test has teeth.  If the matter metric carried a DISFORMAL piece,
         g_mu_nu = e^(2w) gtilde_mu_nu + B T_mu T_nu, the clock WOULD appear in S_m explicitly.
           conformal metric:  g^ab dpsi_a dpsi_b = (a_0**2 - a_1**2)*exp(-2*w);   d/dT_0 = 0
           disformal metric:  d/dT_0 = -2*B*a_0**2*t_0/(B*t_0**2 + exp(2*w))**2  -- NONZERO, a vertex proportional to
           T^mu T^nu dpsi_mu dpsi_nu, i.e. to T^mu_nu contracted with the clock gradient, NOT to the trace.
  [PASS] C4 [coupling] IC10's clock couples to ordinary matter ONLY through the conformal factor -- the vertex is to T^mu_mu and to nothing else -- and the test detects a direct coupling when one is present (disformal positive control)   (dS_m/dw = -sqrt(-g) T^mu_mu exactly; the clock field T is absent from S_m for the conformal metric (d/dT_0 = 0) and present for a disformal one (d/dT_0 = -2*B*a_0**2*t_0/(B*t_0**2 + exp(2*w))**2), so the check is not vacuous.  ASSUMPTIONS, stated: (i) IC10's S_m[e^(2w)gtilde,psi] is the complete matter action -- IC10 itself flags 'matter-coupled clock kinetic mixing must still be varied'; (ii) w remains auxiliary (P_ww != 0), which L8-P6/P7/P8 verified on the sampled window)

    C.3  WHY THIS IS THE WHOLE QUESTION.  In Einstein-aether / khronometric theory -- which is what
         IC5/IC6/IC7 were, and what Elliott, Moore & Stoica (2005) bound -- the spin-0 mode lives
         INSIDE the gravitational sector: the aether supplies a preferred vector u_mu, the scalar's
         effective polarisation carries a u_mu u_nu piece, and e_mu_nu T^mu_nu then picks out
         T^00 ~ E^2.  That is why the spin-0 bound is as strong as the spin-2 one.  On IC10's eta = 1
         plateau there is no such structure: L8 verified the DeWitt supermetric is lambda = 1
         IDENTICALLY and m* = m e^(-1/6) > 0, so the graviton kinetic operator is Einstein's, with no
         preferred-frame mixing to inherit.  The clock's polarisation is a SCALAR: it contracts with
         g_mu_nu, not with u_mu u_nu.  The mode's character genuinely changed, and with it the vertex.

======================================================================================================================
D -- the bound a TRACE-coupled clock at gravitational strength actually has to satisfy
======================================================================================================================
    The on-shell trace vertices, all three derived above:
      Dirac primary (a proton):   <p'|T^mu_mu|p> = M ubar(p')u(p);  Sum/2 = 2M^2 (p.p' + M^2)
                                  with p.p' = M^2 + (1-v^2)k^2/2   =>  M^2 [4M^2 + (1-v^2)k^2]
      spin-0 primary:             <T^mu_mu> = 2M^2 + k^2_inv = 2M^2 - (1-v^2)k^2
      photon:                     <T^mu_mu> = 0 identically (C2)
      massless conformal scalar:  0 (the xi = 1/6 improvement cancels the k^2 piece)

    D.1  THE NAIVE ARGUMENT, TESTED AND CORRECTED.  A conformally coupled scalar is usually said to
         decouple from ultra-relativistic matter because T^mu_mu = -M^2/E per particle, a (M/E)^2
         suppression relative to the graviton's T^00 ~ E.  That is TRUE for soft/forward emission and
         FALSE at Cherenkov kinematics, because the emitted quantum is spacelike with |q^2| =
         (1-v^2)k^2, and the trace vertex picks that up.  This lane checked it rather than assuming it:
         E = 1e20 eV, c_s^2 = 1/3, at k = k_max/2:  |M_trace|^2/|M_tensor|^2 = 8.255e-23,   naive (M/E)^4 = 7.750e-45   (naive too small by 9.4e-23x)
         E = 3e20 eV, c_s^2 = 1/3, at k = k_max/2:  |M_trace|^2/|M_tensor|^2 = 9.172e-24,   naive (M/E)^4 = 9.568e-47   (naive too small by 1.0e-23x)
  [FAIL] C5 [vertex] the trace vertex is (M/E)^2-suppressed relative to the tensor vertex at Cherenkov kinematics, as the standard conformal-decoupling argument would have it   (it is NOT: the true ratio of |M|^2 at c_s^2 = 1/3 is 9.17e-24, the naive (M/E)^4 is 9.57e-47, too small by 1.0e-23x.  The reason is the on-shell identity 2p.k = k^2 of C1: for a SUBLUMINAL mode the emitted quantum is spacelike, so p.p' = M^2 + (1-v^2)k^2/2 grows with k and the trace does not vanish.  This FAIL is this lane correcting its own first-pass reasoning, and it cuts AGAINST the clock -- the suppression is real but far smaller than 'conformal scalars do not couple to radiation' suggests)

    D.2  the bound.  Coupling L = (beta/M_Pl) phi T^mu_mu, beta = 1 the gravitational-strength
         benchmark (Brans-Dicke has beta = 1/sqrt(2 omega+3); f(R) has beta = 1/sqrt6 = 0.408).
         Analytic small-delta limit of the same integral:  D_loss^-1 = (1/4) beta^2 G M^2 delta E,
         LINEAR in delta, not quadratic -- so the bound scales quite differently from the tensor one.
           primary / energy                            bound on 1 - c_s (beta = 1)     vs L10 2e-15
           proton (Dirac), E = 1e20 eV                                   4.331e-09         2.2e+06x
           proton (Dirac), E = 3e20 eV                                   1.444e-09         7.2e+05x
           spin-0 primary (not physical), E = 3e20 eV                    1.029e-16         5.1e-02x
         Note the third row, and it is not a rounding coincidence: for a SPIN-0 primary the trace
         coupling gives NUMERICALLY THE SAME bound as the tensor coupling treated the same way
         (1.029e-16 vs the coherent tensor bound 1.029e-16) -- both integrals reduce to
         (2/3) delta^2 E^6 in the small-delta limit.  So the suppression found here is NOT a generic
         property of conformal coupling: it is entirely the DIRAC structure <T^mu_mu> = M ubar(p')u(p),
         which carries one power of M^2 that the spin-0 trace 2M^2 - (1-v^2)k^2 does not.  The physical
         primary is a proton, so the fermionic row is the one that counts -- but the mechanism has to be
         named correctly, and 'conformal scalars decouple from radiation' is NOT the mechanism.
         No partonic factor is applied here, and that is deliberate: the trace charge of a proton is
         its MASS (<p|T^mu_mu|p> = 2M_p^2 exactly for the hadron state), so the coherent treatment is
         the right one.  The proton's trace form factor at |q^2| ~ (3.1e+11 GeV)^2 would
         suppress it enormously further; omitting it is CONSERVATIVE, i.e. generous to the exclusion.

         At the measured c_s^2 = 1/3 (1 - c_s = 0.4226), E = 3e+11 GeV:
           trace coupling  (beta = 1):  D_loss = 5.171e+11 m = 1.676e-08 kpc  -> D/D_loss = 5.97e+08
           tensor coupling (L10's):     D_loss = 1.489e-11 m = 4.825e-31 kpc  -> D/D_loss = 2.07e+31
           the trace coupling weakens the LOSS RATE by 3.47e+22x and the SPEED BOUND by 7.22e+05x
  [FAIL] C6 [bound] 1 - c_s <= 2e-15, the number L10 imposed, is the bound that applies to IC10's clock   (it is NOT.  The bound that applies to a trace-coupled clock at gravitational strength is 1 - c_s <= 1.44e-09 at 3e20 eV / 4.33e-09 at 1e20 eV -- L10's number is too tight by 7.2e+05x in the speed, equivalently 3.5e+22x in the loss rate AT c_s^2 = 1/3 (the two differ because the tensor rate goes as delta^2 and the trace rate as delta).  L10's stated exclusion factor 2.1e14 should read 2.9e+08.  THIS FAIL IS L10 BEING CORRECTED, and the correction is 5.9 orders of magnitude -- but see C8a: it does not by itself save c_s^2 = 1/3)

======================================================================================================================
E -- where the exponential wall is actually evaluated: the MOND radius of the cosmic ray
======================================================================================================================
    L10 section D asked whether the construction's own screening u^2 = 1 - exp(-|a|/a_0) could
    suppress the Cherenkov coupling, evaluated it at the AMBIENT galactic acceleration along the
    10 kpc path (|a| ~ a_0 there), and concluded no.  That is the wrong place to evaluate it, and
    the reason is published: Milgrom, 'Gravitational Cherenkov losses in MOND theories',
    arXiv:1102.1818 (2011).  Radiation of wavenumber k is generated coherently at distance ~1/k
    from the primary, and THERE the field is the primary's OWN, not the Galaxy's.  A high-energy
    hadron carries a bubble of radius r_M = (G p / c a_0)^(1/2) inside which |a| >> a_0 and a
    GR-compatible MOND theory is simply GR; waves with k > 1/r_M are not produced.

    E.1  the numbers, on BOTH footings (M_p = 0.9383 GeV):
      footing        E [eV]      r_M [m]   k_dB^-1 [m]   (r_M k_dB)^2  |a| at k_dB^-1 / a_0   l_M = c^2/a_0 [m]
      canonical     1.0e+20   1.1273e-08    1.9733e-27     3.2638e+37            3.2638e+37          9.6001e+26
      canonical     3.0e+20   1.9526e-08    6.5776e-28     8.8122e+38            8.8122e+38          9.6001e+26
      alt           1.0e+20   1.0271e-08    1.9733e-27     2.7090e+37            2.7090e+37          7.9684e+26
      alt           3.0e+20   1.7789e-08    6.5776e-28     7.3143e+38            7.3143e+38          7.9684e+26
      columns 5 and 6 are equal BY THE IDENTITY a(r)/a_0 = (r_M/r)^2 -- that is what r_M means.
      Milgrom's quoted values, for comparison: r_M ~ 3e-12 (cp/GeV)^(1/2) cm = 1.643e-08 m at 3e20 eV; (r_M k_dB)^2 ~ 1e39 at cp = 3e11 GeV;
      l_M ~ 2 pi D_H = 8.168e+26 m.  Milgrom uses a_0 = 1.2e-10 m/s^2; rescaling his r_M to
      the canonical footing gives 1.860e-08 m, 5% from this lane's value -- the residual is his ~ sign.

    E.2  CONTROL -- feeding that cutoff into the SAME rate formula reproduces Milgrom's result
         independently.  For k << k_max, sin^2(theta) -> 1 - v^2, so with k_cut = 1/r_M and
         k_cut^2 = a_0/(G E) (c = 1):
             D_loss^-1 = (1/2) G E (1-v^2)^2 k_cut^2 = (1/2)(1-v^2)^2 a_0
             => D_loss  = q l_M   with   q = 2/(1-v^2)^2   and   l_M = c^2/a_0,
         E CANCELS EXACTLY -- Milgrom's eq. (4), derived here from scratch.  Numerically:
      footing        E [eV]  q predicted   q measured    D_loss [m]   D_loss/10kpc   D_loss/D_H
      canonical     1.0e+20       4.5000       4.4998    4.3199e+27     1.4000e+07   3.3230e+01
      canonical     3.0e+20       4.5000       4.4998    4.3199e+27     1.4000e+07   3.3230e+01
      alt           1.0e+20       4.5000       4.4998    3.5856e+27     1.1620e+07   2.7582e+01
      alt           3.0e+20       4.5000       4.4998    3.5856e+27     1.1620e+07   2.7582e+01
  [PASS] C7 [CONTROL] the derived rate, cut off at the primary's MOND radius, reproduces Milgrom 2011 eq. (4) D_loss = q l_M with the cosmic-ray energy cancelling exactly, q = 2/(1-v^2)^2 -- on both a_0 footings   (predicted q = 2/(1-c_s^2)^2 = 4.500 at c_s^2 = 1/3; measured from the full integral at all four (footing, energy) combinations to within 40%; E cancels because k_cut^2 = a_0/(G E) exactly undoes the E-dependence of the rate.  This is an INDEPENDENT re-derivation of a published, refereed result, from the same machinery that reproduced Moore & Nelson in C3)

    E.3  the framework's own wall is STRONGER than Milgrom's sharp cutoff.  IC5/IC10 define
         w = (u-1) xi, and the static regular branch (IC-4, retained at eta = 0: 'the old exponential
         constitutive equation therefore survives') has u^2 = 1 - exp(-|a|/a_0), so
             1 - u  ~  (1/2) exp(-|a|/a_0)   =>   w ~ -(xi/2) exp(-|a|/a_0)   =>   e^(2w) -> 1
         and the ENTIRE clock-matter vertex, which is carried by w alone (C.1), switches off
         exponentially in |a|/a_0.  In the cosmic ray's own near field at the de Broglie radius,
             canonical : |a|/a_0 = 8.812e+38   =>   coupling ~ exp(-8.81e+38)
             alt       : |a|/a_0 = 7.314e+38   =>   coupling ~ exp(-7.31e+38)
         which is not a factor of 1e-39, it is zero for every purpose.  The residual emission comes
         only from k < 1/r_M, and E.2 shows that is D_loss ~ 4.5 l_M -- longer than a Hubble
         distance.  Note this is with the FULL tensor coupling; the trace coupling of section D makes
         it 3e+22x safer still.

======================================================================================================================
F -- the verdict on c_s^2 = 1/3, arm by arm
======================================================================================================================
    F.1  trace coupling alone, NO screening, point-like proton, beta = 1, 10 kpc:
           D/D_loss = 5.968e+08   ->  c_s^2 = 1/3 is EXCLUDED by this arm alone, by 5.97e+08x in the loss rate
           (required suppression of the coupling: beta^2 <= 1.68e-09, i.e. beta <= 4.09e-05)
  [FAIL] C8a [verdict] c_s^2 = 1/3 survives gravitational Cherenkov on the CORRECTED coupling alone, with no screening applied   (it does not: D/D_loss = 5.97e+08 at beta = 1.  The corrected bound is 1 - c_s <= 1.44e-09, and 1 - c_s = 0.4226 exceeds it by 2.9e+08x.  So L10's CONCLUSION survives on this arm even though its NUMBER was wrong by 7.2e+05x)

    F.2  with the framework's own high-acceleration GR limit applied where the radiation is generated:
      footing        E [eV]  coupling    D_loss [m]   D_loss / 10 kpc    verdict
      canonical     1.0e+20    tensor    4.3199e+27        1.4000e+07       SAFE
      canonical     1.0e+20     trace    1.2386e+71        4.0141e+50       SAFE
      canonical     3.0e+20    tensor    4.3199e+27        1.4000e+07       SAFE
      canonical     3.0e+20     trace    1.0033e+73        3.2515e+52       SAFE
      alt           1.0e+20    tensor    3.5856e+27        1.1620e+07       SAFE
      alt           1.0e+20     trace    1.0281e+71        3.3319e+50       SAFE
      alt           3.0e+20    tensor    3.5856e+27        1.1620e+07       SAFE
      alt           3.0e+20     trace    8.3276e+72        2.6988e+52       SAFE
  [PASS] C8b [verdict] c_s^2 = 1/3 survives gravitational Cherenkov once the high-acceleration GR limit is applied where the radiation is generated (Milgrom 2011), on BOTH a_0 footings and BOTH couplings   (yes, and not marginally: even with the FULL gravitational-strength tensor coupling the loss distance is 4.5 l_M, 1.40e+07x the Galactic path and 33.2x the Hubble distance.  With the trace coupling of section D it is safer by a further 3e+22x.  Footing-independent)

======================================================================================================================
G -- the other constraints that could bite on a subluminal, minimally coupled clock
======================================================================================================================
    G.1  CAUSALITY.  Subluminal is the easy direction: the clock cone lies strictly INSIDE the metric
         null cone, so the causal future of the coupled system is contained in the metric light cone
         and no closed causal curve can be built from it.  The preferred foliation is not even needed
         for this -- it is needed only for the SUPERLUMINAL case, where the clock's own level sets
         T = const provide a global time function that all cones respect.  Worth recording because
         L8-D7 found a superluminal band 0.0267 < S < 0.0377 inside eta = 1, in this solution's PAST:
         that band needs the foliation argument, this one does not.
  [PASS] C9 [causality] the subluminal clock cone is causal -- it lies inside the metric null cone, so no closed causal curve can be formed   (c_s = 0.577350 < 1, cone strictly inside; the preferred foliation is a spare argument here, and is the one that would be needed for L8-D7's superluminal band at S < 0.0377)

    G.2  OTHER EMITTERS.  Cherenkov by something other than a hadron:
      photons:    T^mu_mu = 0 identically in 4D (C2), so a photon CANNOT emit a conformally coupled
                  clock quantum at tree level, at any speed.  LHAASO's PeV photons give nothing.
      neutrinos:  the vertex is proportional to m_nu^2; against a proton the rate is down by
                  (m_nu/M_p)^4 = 1.29e-40.  IceCube's PeV events give nothing.
      gravitons:  the tensor mode is exactly luminal here (c_T = 1, an exact action identity, IC9/IC10,
                  verified L8-A2/A3), so the channel Elliott, Moore & Stoica actually bound is closed at
                  zero.  A graviton could emit a clock quantum only via the background clock gradient
                  Tbar_mu, and there is no observed ultra-high-energy graviton flux to constrain it
                  (GW170817's quanta are ~1e-13 eV).  Vacuous, not passed.
  [FAIL] C10 [channels] photons, neutrinos or gravitons impose a Cherenkov bound on this clock that is stronger than the hadronic one   (none of them do: photon vertex identically zero (Maxwell trace = 8.9e-16), neutrino rate down by 1.3e-40, no UHE graviton flux exists.  This FAIL means 'no additional constraint found', which is the honest reading -- the hadronic channel is the binding one and it is the one computed above)

    G.3  BINARY PULSARS.  A conformally coupled scalar generically radiates DIPOLE energy from an
         asymmetric binary, and PSR J0348+0432 / J1738+0333 bound the scalar-charge difference at the
         |alpha_A - alpha_B|^2 ~ 1e-6 level.  Two things are true and both must be said:
           (i) this is a HIGH-acceleration system, so the same exponential wall applies, and it applies
               HERE at the right place (the binary's own field, not an ambient one).  Milgrom 2011 makes
               exactly this point: 'constraints on solar-system and binary-pulsar limits on preferred-
               frame parameters are easily satisfied by GR-compatible MOND theories, inasmuch as these
               limits pertain to experiments in high-acceleration systems'.  L10's own K6 is the same
               statement in the Solar System (Cassini at 5.8e5 a_0).
          (ii) the dipole coefficient is NOT computed here and cannot be, because IC10's clock-matter
               sector is one of its own stated open items ('matter-coupled clock kinetic mixing must
               still be varied').  It is a live constraint, not a passed one.
    G.4  CMB.  c_s^2 = 1/3 is exactly the radiation sound speed; a component with that sound speed does
         not cluster below its sound horizon and its perturbations are CMB-degenerate with a smooth
         relativistic fluid.  The CMB constrains the clock's ENERGY DENSITY (N_eff), not its speed --
         which is L10's K5b arm, unchanged and still open.  No bound on c_s from the CMB.
